A mutable collection you use to temprorarily store transient key-value pairs that are subject to eviction when resources are low. 

1. When you will use NSCache?
You typically use NSCache objects to temporarily store objects with transient data that are expensive to create. 
Reusing these objects can provide performance benefits, because their values do not have to be recalculated. However, the objects 
are not critical to the application and can be discarded if memory is tight. If discarded, their values will have to be recomputed again when needed. 

2. How NSCache objects provide the performance benefits?
Unlike an NSMutableDictionary object, a cache does not copy the key objects that are put into it. 

3. How NSCache make sure it does not use much of system memory?
The NSCache class incorporates various auto-eviction policies, which ensures that a cache doesn't use too much of the system's memory. If memory is needed 
by the other applications, these policies remove some items from the cache, minimizing its memory footprint. 

4. Can use different threads with NSCache?
Yes. You can add, remove, and query items in the cache from different threads without having to lock the cache yourself. 

5. How NSDiscardableContent protocol helps to improve cache eviction behaviour?
Objects that have subcomponents that can be discarded when not being used can adopt the NSDiscardableContent protocol to improve cache eviction behaviour. 
By default, NSDiscardableContent objects in a cache are automatically removed if their content is discarded, although this autmatic removal policy can be changed.

6. What's difference between NSCache and NSMutableDictionary?
1. Unlike an NSMutableDictionary object, a cache does not copy the key objects that are put into it. 
2. iOS will automatically remove objects from cache if the device is running low on memory. 

7. How system removes items from NSCache. 
Helpfully, if the system does encounter memory pressure NSCache will automatically start to remove items without you knowing about it, 
which means you won't get a memory warning unless even more RAM needs to be cleared. 
It will also remove items intelligently, trying to keep as much cached as possible.

8. How do you make feel that app has improved performance? 
Making app feel fast and responsive - 
1. about tweaking the way its UI is rendered, 
2. improving the sheer execution speed of its operations and algorithms. 
3. Efficiently managing its data and avoiding unnecessary work. 

Unnecessary work such as, we end up reloading the exact same data multiple times. It could be multiple features loading 
duplicate copies of the same model, or view's data being reloaded every time that it reappears on the screen. 

9. What is caching?
- Efficiently store and load values.
- Decide when to evict enteries in order to keep our memory footprint low, invalidate stale data. 

10. How Apple supports caching by its foundation framework?
Built-in NSCache class.

11. What are the drawback of using NSCache?
It's an Objective-C class on Apple's own platforms - which means that it can only store class instances, its only compatible with NSObject- based keys. 

// To be able to use strings as caching keys, we have to use
